The way to use this is very simple and kinda annoying.
Step 1.) Turn on the Pi, if it's plugged in and not on, unplug it and plug it back in
Step 2.) You will need a monitor, keyboard, and mouse to start up the program, the mouse might be optional but I wouldn't push it
Step 3.) Go to the terminal and type "sudo -E python3 -u ~/2026-AttendanceKiosk/code.py", now you can unplug the monitor, keyboard, and mouse
Step 4.) Now scan the badges and sign people in
Step 5.) To end the script plug in the keyboard and press "q"
Step 6.) Now you can just unplug the pi, or plug in monitor and mouse to type "sudo shutdown now" before unplugging

The full steps for everything below are in docs/pi-setup.md, this is the short version.
If there is no ~/2026-AttendanceKiosk folder on the Pi yet, do the "First time" part of docs/pi-setup.md before Step 3.

Updating the code
The program runs from a copy of the GitHub repo at ~/2026-AttendanceKiosk, so new code comes from GitHub, not a USB stick.
Never edit the code on the Pi. Change it on a laptop and push it to GitHub, then pull it onto the Pi.
When there is new code, before Step 3, go to the terminal and type "cd ~/2026-AttendanceKiosk", then "git status". It should say "nothing to commit, working tree clean"
Then type "git pull" to get the latest code
After updating, scan the test badges to make sure it still works
If the new code doesn't work, type "git log --oneline -10" to find the last version that did, then "git checkout" and that version's code (the 7 letters and numbers at the start of its line). "git checkout main" goes back to the latest

Putting a new key on the Pi
The key is NOT in the GitHub repo. It stays at ~/QR_reader/service_account.json, and you only need to do this when the key is replaced.
Google names the downloaded file something like "project-name-123456-a1b2c3d4e5f6.json", so rename it to service_account.json when you copy it onto a USB stick.
Plug in the stick and move the old key out of the way: type "mkdir -p ~/QR_reader/old", then "mv ~/QR_reader/service_account.json ~/QR_reader/old/"
Copy the new key in: "cp /media/pihirobotics/STICK/service_account.json ~/QR_reader/" (STICK is the stick's name, type "ls /media/pihirobotics" to see it)
To check it worked, type "ls ~/QR_reader" and look for service_account.json
If the key is missing or misnamed, the program stops right away with "FileNotFoundError" and nothing lights up
When it starts, it should say "Loaded" and a number of badge IDs, then scan the test badges to make sure it works
If the new key doesn't work, put the old one back: "mv ~/QR_reader/old/service_account.json ~/QR_reader/"
Delete the key from the USB stick when you're done. Never copy the key into the GitHub repo, it works like a password for the attendance sheet
